  ---\
  Investigation Report: SMB Output Quality\
\
  The Scale of the Problem\
\
  The Burris Chalmers run produced 15 markdown documents totaling ~280KB / 4,200 lines of content. That's\
  roughly a 150-page document. For a 1-3 person communications firm in WNC.\
\
  What's Actually Happening\
\
  The audience system IS having some effect. The output does reference WNC context, mentions specific budget\
   numbers ($20/month), and uses some accessible language. But it's like putting a friendly label on a\
  battleship \'97 the structure underneath is still pure enterprise.\
\
  The core problem is the prompts themselves. Every prompt template in strategy_factory/synthesis/prompts/\
  demands enterprise-grade structure:\
\
  - quick_wins.py asks for 10 quick wins with comparison matrices, implementation sequences, resource\
  pooling, escalation paths, and success tracking dashboards\
  - change_management.py asks for ADKAR models, stakeholder matrices, communication calendars, 4-level\
  training curricula, and a champions program\
  - vendor_comparison.py asks for enterprise platform comparisons (Azure, AWS), TCO analysis, and\
  build-vs-buy frameworks\
  - data_governance.py asks for governance vision statements, data stewardship roles, and compliance\
  frameworks\
\
  The tone guidance says "accessible, not technical" but the prompt template says "include a stakeholder\
  matrix and ADKAR model application." The prompt template wins that fight every time. Gemini fills in the\
  enterprise template with slightly friendlier language, but the structure is identical to what it would\
  produce for a Fortune 500 company.\
\
  Specific Problems in the Output\
\
  1. Fictional tech stack \'97 The tech inventory lists HubSpot, Asana/ClickUp, Slack/Teams, QuickBooks,\
  WordPress, Backblaze... all guessed. It says so: "Assumptions are made based on typical small business\
  adoption." This is useless for a company-specific strategy.\
  2. Enterprise frameworks everywhere \'97 ADKAR, KPMG TACO, maturity models, governance structures. A solo\
  operator or 3-person firm doesn't need a "Data Lead," "Domain Stewards," or a "4-level training\
  curriculum."\
  3. Make.com / Zapier recommendations \'97 As you noted, not what you'd recommend for these clients.\
  4. Massive redundancy \'97 ChatGPT Plus is recommended in at least 5 documents. The same WNC framing appears\
  in every executive summary. The same 2-3 tool names recur everywhere.\
  5. Questionable value in several documents \'97 "License Consolidation" for a company with maybe 5 SaaS\
  subscriptions? "Data Governance Framework" with org charts? "Change Management Playbook" with\
  communication calendars for a team that probably sits in the same room?\
  6. The SMB guides are better but can't overcome the prompts \'97 The 3 SMB guides (smb-ai-playbook.md,\
  smb-ai-value-playbook.md, ai-implementation-steps-smb.md) are actually decent. They have relevant\
  frameworks like the "80/15/5 Rule" and "BCG's 10-20-70 Rule." But they're injected as "Consulting\
  Knowledge Base" context alongside the prompt templates that still demand enterprise structure.\
\
  Your Options (ranked by impact vs. effort)\
\
  Option A: Add a "Plain English Action Plan" deliverable (new 16th doc)\
\
  Add a post-processing step that synthesizes all 15 documents into a 3-5 page plain-language brief:\
  - "3 things to do this month, here's exactly how"\
  - "What it costs (total: $X/month)"\
  - "What you get (hours saved, revenue gained)"\
  - "Step-by-step: open this tool, type this prompt, get this result"\
\
  This is the "hand the client something they'll actually read" option. Doesn't change any existing\
  deliverables. One new prompt, one new deliverable entry in config. Effort: Low. Impact: High.\
\
  Option B: Rewrite the prompts to be audience-aware\
\
  Add conditional instructions to each prompt so that when an SMB audience is active, the structure changes\
  dramatically \'97 shorter documents, fewer sections, no enterprise frameworks, concrete tool names with\
  prices, "do this, not that" format.\
\
  The current prompt templates are the root cause. They're 100% enterprise regardless of audience. This\
  would fix that at the source. Effort: Medium-High (15 prompts to revise). Impact: Very High.\
\
  Option C: Reduce deliverable count for SMB audiences\
\
  When an SMB audience is active, skip the less relevant documents entirely. For a small business, these are\
   probably unnecessary:\
  - 07_vendor_comparison (they're not comparing Azure vs AWS)\
  - 08_license_consolidation (they have 5 subscriptions, not 50)\
  - 11_data_governance (org chart governance is meaningless)\
  - 13_glossary (they can Google terms)\
  - 15_change_management (ADKAR for a 3-person team is absurd)\
\
  This reduces the output from 15 docs to ~10, which is still a lot but less overwhelming. Effort: Low.\
  Impact: Medium.\
\
  Option D: Create a parallel "SMB track" with different deliverables entirely\
\
  Instead of the current 15 enterprise deliverables, create a separate set of 5-6 SMB-specific deliverables:\
  1. Where You Stand (simplified assessment)\
  2. What To Do First (3 quick wins with exact steps)\
  3. The Plan (plain language 30/60/90 day roadmap)\
  4. What It Costs (budget breakdown with specific tools and prices)\
  5. Cheat Sheet (prompts and tool tips they can use today)\
\
  This would be a separate config and prompt set, reusing the same research/synthesis infrastructure.\
  Effort: High. Impact: Very High.\
\
  My Recommendation\
\
  Do Option A first, then Option B. Option A gives you something immediately usable \'97 a short document you\
  can actually hand to a client. Option B fixes the root cause over time.\
\
  Option D is the right long-term architecture, but it's a bigger project. Options A+B together get you 80%\
  of the value for 20% of the effort.\
\
