  ---\
  The Consulting Guide Problem\
\
  This is the harder problem, and you've already discovered why: there genuinely isn't much good\
  SMB-specific AI content out there. Most of what exists falls into three categories:\
\
  1. Enterprise content with "SMB" in the title \'97 Repackaged McKinsey/BCG frameworks. "Here's how to\
  implement AI" that assumes a CTO, a data team, and a $500K budget.\
  2. Tool vendor marketing \'97 "How to use AI in your small business" that's really a ChatGPT sales pitch or a\
   Zapier tutorial. No strategic framework, just "buy our thing."\
  3. Surface-level blog posts \'97 "10 AI tools for small business" listicles. No depth, no methodology, no\
  implementation guidance.\
\
  The three guides you found (smb-ai-playbook.md, smb-ai-value-playbook.md, ai-implementation-steps-smb.md)\
  are better than most \'97 they have real frameworks (BCG 10-20-70, the 80/15/5 rule) and actual statistics.\
  But they're still limited, and as you noted, they took significant effort to find.\
\
  For an SMB app, you probably need a different approach to the knowledge base\
\
  Instead of loading consulting firm summaries (which are all enterprise by nature), consider:\
\
  Option 1: Build a curated tool/practice database instead of consulting guides\
  - A structured knowledge base of: tool name, price, what it does, who it's for, how to set it up\
  - Specific prompts and workflows for common SMB tasks (social media, email, invoicing, scheduling)\
  - Case studies from actual small businesses (not Fortune 500)\
\
  This is more like a "practitioner's reference" than a consulting guide. You could build it from real-world\
   use, your own coaching experience, and community resources.\
\
  Option 2: Use live web research instead of static guides\
  - The current app uses Perplexity for research, then loads static consulting guides into the prompt\
  - For SMB, you could run additional Perplexity queries at synthesis time: "What are the best AI tools\
  under $30/month for [specific task] in 2026?"\
  - This keeps recommendations current without needing to maintain static guides\
\
  Option 3: Inject your own expertise as the "guide"\
  - You're the AI adoption coach. Your knowledge and judgment about what works for small businesses is the\
  actual differentiator.\
  - Write a set of "coach notes" \'97 not consulting firm summaries, but practical guidance from your\
  experience\
  - Things like: "Don't recommend Make.com to a non-technical owner. Recommend Zapier if they need\
  automation, but honestly most SMBs should start with native integrations in tools they already have."\
  - This would be more opinionated and more useful than any consulting guide.\
\
  Option 3 is probably the most valuable because it captures YOUR expertise, which is the thing that can't\
  be replicated by another AI tool. The consulting guides in the current app are essentially commodity\
  knowledge \'97 any AI can read a McKinsey report. But your specific recommendations about what works for a\
  3-person cleaning company in WNC \'97 that's unique.\
