  What the Rewritten Prompts Should Focus On\
\
  Based on what you described as the real SMB pain points, the prompts should center on these specific task\
  areas:\
\
  1. Voice AI / Chatbot \'97 "Can AI answer my phone / website chat so I stop missing leads?"\
  2. Email sorting & responding \'97 "Can AI help me get through my inbox faster?"\
  3. Scheduling \'97 "Can AI book appointments without the back-and-forth?"\
  4. Content production \'97 "Can AI write my social media posts / blog / newsletter?"\
  5. Website / SEO \'97 "Can AI tell me what's wrong with my website and how to fix it?"\
  6. Proposals & quotes \'97 "Can AI help me respond to leads faster with better proposals?"\
  7. Invoicing & follow-up \'97 "Can AI help me get paid faster?"\
  8. Customer communication \'97 "Can AI help me stay in touch with customers without spending hours?"\
\
  These are things a small business owner wakes up worrying about. Not "data governance maturity" or\
  "license consolidation opportunities."\
\
  How to Implement Options A+B+C Together\
\
  Option C (reduce deliverables): Skip the 9 Tier 3 prompts when an SMB audience is active. This alone cuts\
  output from ~280KB to ~100KB.\
\
  Option B (rewrite prompts): Rewrite the 6 Tier 1+2 prompts to be SMB-focused. These are the prompts that\
  actually drive value. The rewrites aren't tweaks \'97 they're replacements. Different sections, different\
  language, different structure.\
\
  Option A (add summary): After the 6 rewritten deliverables are generated, run one final synthesis pass\
  that produces a 2-3 page "Here's Your Action Plan" \'97 the one document you actually hand to the client.\
\
  The Good News\
\
  The prompt rewrites are straightforward work. The current prompts are all in\
  strategy_factory/synthesis/prompts/ as Python files containing a single PROMPT string. Each one is\
  self-contained. Rewriting a prompt doesn't touch any other code \'97 no orchestrator changes, no config\
  changes (for the content), no pipeline changes.\
\
  The harder part is the config: making the system skip deliverables conditionally based on audience. That's\
   a code change in config.py and potentially in the synthesis orchestrator to filter the deliverable list.\
\
  The Specific Tool Problem\
\
  You mentioned not wanting to recommend Make.com, Azure, AWS, etc. The current prompts are vendor-agnostic\
  by design \'97 they ask Gemini to "recommend tools" without guidance. Gemini defaults to the most commonly\
  mentioned tools in its training data, which means Zapier, Make.com, Azure, etc.\
\
  The fix is to be opinionated in the prompts. Instead of "recommend AI/automation tools," say things like:\
\
  \uc0\u9614  "Recommend specific tools the owner can start using today. Prefer tools that are simple to set up\
  without technical knowledge. Avoid recommending complex automation platforms (Zapier, Make.com) unless the\
   business has someone technical on staff. Focus on tools with free tiers or simple monthly pricing under\
  $30/month. Good examples: ChatGPT Plus for content and email, Calendly for scheduling, Canva for design,\
  Google's built-in AI features."\
\
  This is where your own expertise becomes the guide \'97 you can inject your preferences directly into the\
  prompts. The consulting guides don't need to tell Gemini what to recommend when YOU can tell it directly\
  through the prompt.\
